{
  "_comment": "The device UDP test a person runs (customer request, 28 October 2026): its length is chosen between min_seconds and max_seconds (default_seconds unless chosen), at a pace the server sets - default_rate_pps unless UDP_TEST_RATE_PPS says otherwise, never above max_rate_pps - and the person cannot change. setup_allowance_ms is the time a session may spend before its first probe and after its last (gathering, connecting, the handshake, the warm-up echo, the drain and the summary). The Go reflector constants (reflectorconstants) are these; every reflector's limits are tested to hold max_seconds at max_rate_pps plus setup_allowance_ms; and the app's engine is tested to spend no more than setup_allowance_ms outside sending.",
  "min_seconds": 30,
  "max_seconds": 300,
  "default_seconds": 60,
  "default_rate_pps": 10,
  "max_rate_pps": 20,
  "setup_allowance_ms": 40000,
  "_speed": "The line's speed, measured after the test to the same reflector over the same connection (customer request, November 2026): numbered packets are sent each way for speed_default_seconds unless UDP_TEST_SPEED_SECONDS says otherwise - 0 measures none - and never for more than speed_max_seconds, which every reflector that measures speed allows. speed_pacing is how a sender paces them from what its receiver counts - the reflector for a download (reflectorcore/ReflectorSpeed.go), the page's engine for an upload (packetpulse-udp.js) - and cases are counts with the pace a sender must be at after each: both implementations are run through them, so the two cannot come to pace differently. Each case's step is a count the receiver said - packets, the highest index, ms - with the sender's next index, whether it was held back (held_back), and the least its probes waited in the queue in front of the line meanwhile (queued_ms, absent where nothing read it).",
